6. ADMINISTRATOR RIGHTS AND THE RAVE ANTICHEAT

6.1 Rave asks Windows for administrator rights when it starts. This is needed for the built-in anticheat and for parts of Rave that work with other processes on your computer. If you decline, Rave closes and does not run.

6.2 Rave version 0.1 does not contain an active anticheat yet. The anticheat is planned for a later version (currently version 0.2). Nothing in Rave version 0.1 checks for, detects, blocks or reports cheats, and nobody may claim otherwise.

6.3 When an anticheat is part of Rave, it may, while Rave and Roblox are running, look at information on your computer that is needed to find cheating or tampering. This can include, for example, the list of running programs and their names, files and modules that are loaded into Roblox or into Rave, windows that are drawn over Roblox, changes to Rave or Roblox files, and the integrity of Rave itself. The exact checks may change from version to version, and the Developer will describe them in the documentation or release notes of that version. By using a version of Rave that contains the anticheat, you agree to these checks.

6.4 The anticheat does not promise to find every cheat, and a missing detection does not mean that a player is not cheating. It also does not promise that it will never wrongly flag a program. The Developer is not responsible for any decision that you or anyone else makes based on the anticheat.

6.5 The Rave anticheat is a tool of Rave. It is not made by, approved by or connected to Roblox Corporation, and it is not the anticheat of Roblox. Roblox has its own systems, and Rave does not change, replace or weaken them.

6.6 Because Rave runs with administrator rights, you should only run official Rave builds. Running a modified or unofficial build with administrator rights can seriously harm your computer, your files and your accounts. The Developer is not responsible for any version of Rave that was not provided by the Developer.


7. ROBLOX AND OTHER THIRD-PARTY SERVICES

7.1 Rave is an independent project. Rave and the Developer are not affiliated with, endorsed by, sponsored by or connected to Roblox Corporation in any way. "Roblox" and the Roblox logos are trademarks of Roblox Corporation. They are used only to describe what Rave works with.

7.2 Rave does not contain the Roblox client. When you start Roblox from Rave, Rave downloads the official Roblox client directly from the servers of Roblox Corporation, checks it with the checksums that Roblox publishes, places it in the Rave folder on your computer and starts it. The Roblox client, its content and all Roblox services stay the property of Roblox Corporation and are subject only to the terms of Roblox Corporation.

7.3 Your use of Roblox is governed by the Roblox Terms of Use and all other rules of Roblox Corporation, which you accept directly with Roblox Corporation. You are solely responsible for following them. The Developer has no control over Roblox, cannot change your Roblox account, cannot restore bans, cannot recover items, and gives no promise about what Roblox Corporation does.

7.4 Roblox Corporation can change or stop the Roblox client, its download servers, its version service or its rules at any time. This can stop Rave from working, fully or partly, until Rave is updated, or forever. The Developer is not responsible for this.

7.5 FFlags (Fast Flags). Rave contains an FFlags editor that writes settings, which you choose, into a configuration file of the Roblox client. FFlags are internal settings of Roblox. Roblox Corporation decides which FFlags the client accepts, and it can ignore, limit or block them at any time. Changing FFlags can make Roblox unstable, slow, ugly or unusable, and some changes may be against the rules of Roblox or of a Roblox experience. You use the FFlags editor entirely at your own risk and you are solely responsible for every FFlag you set, import or share. If something goes wrong, you can empty the FFlags editor and save it to remove your FFlags.

7.6 Rave can open or use other third-party services, such as GitHub (to show the latest Rave news) and Microsoft (to check whether your computer is online). These services belong to their owners and have their own terms and privacy rules. The Developer is not responsible for them.


8. YOUR DATA AND PRIVACY

8.1 Rave does not have user accounts, does not ask for your Roblox password, does not read your Roblox account data, does not show advertising and does not contain tracking or telemetry. Rave does not send any personal data to the Developer.

8.2 Rave connects to the internet only for its own functions. In version 0.1 these are: a connection check with Microsoft (www.msftconnecttest.com), the Roblox version service and the Roblox download servers (clientsettingscdn.roblox.com and setup.rbxcdn.com), and the GitHub API (api.github.com) to show the latest Rave news. Like any internet connection, these services can see your IP address and that a request was made. Their own privacy rules apply.

8.3 Rave stores its files only on your computer, mainly in the folder %LOCALAPPDATA%\Rave. These are the Roblox client versions that Rave downloaded, your FFlags (fflags.json) and your Rave settings (settings.json). Rave does not write log files.

8.4 If Rave crashes, or if a Roblox download, install or start fails, Rave may create a crash report or a bug report in the folder %TEMP%\rave-temp. These reports can contain your Windows version, whether Rave had administrator rights, technical error details and a memory snapshot of Rave at the moment of the crash. They stay on your computer. They are never sent automatically. You decide whether you want to send them to the Developer, and if you do, you agree that the Developer may use them to find and fix problems.

8.5 You can remove all data that Rave created by uninstalling Rave and choosing to remove the Rave user data, or by deleting the folders %LOCALAPPDATA%\Rave and %TEMP%\rave-temp yourself.

8.6 If a later version of Rave needs to process more or other data (for example for the anticheat), the Developer will describe this in that version and, where the law requires it, ask for your consent first.


